{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794539492631,"sessionID":"ses_e2d1dfe6affeApOQ0VkusgZ7Wu","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2e39308001tgyXu142ldoHpu","sessionID":"ses_e2d1dfe6affeApOQ0VkusgZ7Wu","messageID":"msg_1d2e386ff001Pp3wxe4D6OiRA3","type":"tool","id":"call_e101586ddaf04afb83aa33f0","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/INSTALL-SKILLED.md","offset":45,"limit":70},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/INSTALL-SKILLED.md, lines 45-114\n45: ```\n46: \n47: Then build the Skill Advisor. Its build uses the Spec Kit's TypeScript compiler, so it comes second. The last command checks the advisor's CLI. `list-tools` answers offline, with no daemon running.\n48: \n49: ```bash\n50: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime install\n51: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run build\n52: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs list-tools --format json\n53: ```\n54: \n55: ### Check the Install\n56: \n57: Open the repository in any of the seven runtimes: OpenCode, Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Devin, Pi or DeepSeek Harness (DSH). Then run:\n58: \n59: ```\n60: /doctor:setup\n61: ```\n62: \n63: It checks the install without changing anything. It names one repair command for each failure.\n64: \n65: Codex, Cursor, Pi and DSH spell commands with a hyphen, as in `/doctor-setup`. Devin reaches the spec-kit workflows through its `/system-spec-kit` skill.\n66: \n67: DSH loads the repository's guards and the skill advisor only from the patches a run names, so start it with `--patch .dsh/patches/guards.patch.yml`.\n68: \n69: Codex runs a project hook only after you approve it, so open `/hooks` in a Codex session at the repository root and approve each entry it lists. Codex keeps each approval as a `trusted_hash` in `~/.codex/config.toml`, so a changed entry needs approving again, and `/doctor:runtime-mirrors` prints `PASS codex-hooks-approved` once the advisor's entry is approved.\n70: \n71: ### Meaning Matching for Commands\n72: \n73: Until a local embedder model runs, the advisor matches a typed request to a command by phrase alone. With the model loaded, a command can also match by meaning, even when your request uses none of its words. The model is `nomic-embed-text:v1.5`, served by Ollama on your machine, and the advisor calls it once per recommendation.\n74: \n75: Run the setup from the repository you are setting up:\n76: \n77: ```\n78: /doctor:skill-advisor embedder\n79: ```\n80: \n81: It probes Ollama, the server and the model first, and changes nothing. It then lists each missing step with its cost and its undo, and asks before it acts. Only `approve` proceeds. From the approved plan it pulls the model, starts `ollama serve` in the background and runs `skill_graph_scan` to write a vector for every command entry. It never installs or upgrades Ollama. When Ollama is missing, the plan prints the install command for you to run.\n82: \n83: Measured on 2026-11-13 with Ollama 0.40.15:\n84: \n85: | What it costs | Measured |\n86: | --- | --- |\n87: | Disk for `nomic-embed-text:v1.5` | 274302450 bytes, about 262 MB |\n88: | Memory while `ollama serve` is idle | 25776 KB resident, about 25 MB |\n89: | Memory while the model is loaded | 32208 KB for the server plus 279520 KB for the model runner, about 304 MB together |\n90: | Unload when idle | after five idle minutes without a request |\n91: | Warm embedding latency | 4.8 ms median and 8.9 ms p95 over 30 calls |\n92: | Cold embedding latency | 538.53 ms for the first call |\n93: \n94: Meaning matching is optional. A no at the prompt changes nothing. The install keeps working on phrases alone, and each recommendation that ran without a prompt vector carries `semantic_lane_off:<reason>` in its `warnings`.\n95: \n96: ### Add Code Mode\n97: \n98: Code Mode is the bridge your runtime uses to call external MCP tools. It builds separately from the two builds above. Run:\n99: \n100: ```\n101: /doctor:mcp install\n102: ```\n103: \n104: It installs the dependencies and builds Code Mode when its `dist` is missing or stale. It configures the `.utcp_config.json` manuals. It registers Code Mode in the seven project config files, two of them for Claude. Each write waits for its own approval. Add `--runtime <name>` to register one runtime only: `opencode`, `claude`, `codex`, `cursor`, `pi` or `devin`.\n105: \n106: To check Code Mode later, run `/doctor:mcp debug`. It reports PASS, WARN or FAIL per check, and `--fix` adds guided repair. Provider keys live in `.env`, which only Code Mode reads. Section 1 of [`.env.example`](.env.example) lists them. An agent cannot read that file: the [dotenv guard](.skilled/hooks/env-secret-guard/README.md) refuses it on every runtime and offers key names or a fingerprint instead. The [Code Mode install guide](.skilled/skills/mcp-code-mode/INSTALL-GUIDE.md) covers the manual steps.\n107: \n108: ### Git Hooks\n109: \n110: The first AI session installs the repository's git hooks. The root README's [Git Hooks](README.md#git-hooks) section says what each hook blocks. `/doctor:git setup` checks the hooks and the sk-git settings.\n111: \n112: Your checkout is ready once `/doctor:setup` passes. Run `/doctor:setup` again whenever something looks wrong. To use Skilled in a project of your own, pick a way in below.\n113: \n114: ---\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 115]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/INSTALL-SKILLED.md, lines 45-114\n45: ```\n46: \n47: Then build the Skill Advisor. Its build uses the Spec Kit's TypeScript compiler, so it comes second. The last command checks the advisor's CLI. `list-tools` answers offline, with no daemon running.\n48: \n49: ```bash\n50: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime install\n51: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run build\n52: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs list-tools --format json\n53: ```\n54: \n55: ### Check the Install\n56: \n57: Open the repository in any of the seven runtimes: OpenCode, Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Devin, Pi or DeepSeek Harness (DSH). Then run:\n58: \n59: ```\n60: /doctor:setup\n61: ```\n62: \n63: It checks the install without changing anything. It names one repair command for each failure.\n64: \n65: Codex, Cursor, Pi and DSH spell commands with a hyphen, as in `/doctor-setup`. Devin reaches the spec-kit workflows through its `/system-spec-kit` skill.\n66: \n67: DSH loads the repository's guards and the skill advisor only from the patches a run names, so start it with `--patch .dsh/patches/guards.patch.yml`.\n68: \n69: Codex runs a project hook only after you approve it, so open `/hooks` in a Codex session at the repository root and approve each entry it lists. Codex keeps each approval as a `trusted_hash` in `~/.codex/config.toml`, so a changed entry needs approving again, and `/doctor:runtime-mirrors` prints `PASS codex-hooks-approved` once the advisor's entry is approved.\n70: \n71: ### Meaning Matching for Commands\n72: \n73: Until a local embedder model runs, the advisor matches a typed request to a command by phrase alone. With the model loaded, a command can also match by meaning, even when your request uses none of its words. The model is `nomic-embed-text:v1.5`, served by Ollama on your machine, and the advisor calls it once per recommendation.\n74: \n75: Run the setup from the repository you are setting up:\n76: \n77: ```\n78: /doctor:skill-advisor embedder\n79: ```\n80: \n81: It probes Ollama, the server and the model first, and changes nothing. It then lists each missing step with its cost and its undo, and asks before it acts. Only `approve` proceeds. From the approved plan it pulls the model, starts `ollama serve` in the background and runs `skill_graph_scan` to write a vector for every command entry. It never installs or upgrades Ollama. When Ollama is missing, the plan prints the install command for you to run.\n82: \n83: Measured on 2026-11-13 with Ollama 0.40.15:\n84: \n85: | What it costs | Measured |\n86: | --- | --- |\n87: | Disk for `nomic-embed-text:v1.5` | 274302450 bytes, about 262 MB |\n88: | Memory while `ollama serve` is idle | 25776 KB resident, about 25 MB |\n89: | Memory while the model is loaded | 32208 KB for the server plus 279520 KB for the model runner, about 304 MB together |\n90: | Unload when idle | after five idle minutes without a request |\n91: | Warm embedding latency | 4.8 ms median and 8.9 ms p95 over 30 calls |\n92: | Cold embedding latency | 538.53 ms for the first call |\n93: \n94: Meaning matching is optional. A no at the prompt changes nothing. The install keeps working on phrases alone, and each recommendation that ran without a prompt vector carries `semantic_lane_off:<reason>` in its `warnings`.\n95: \n96: ### Add Code Mode\n97: \n98: Code Mode is the bridge your runtime uses to call external MCP tools. It builds separately from the two builds above. Run:\n99: \n100: ```\n101: /doctor:mcp install\n102: ```\n103: \n104: It installs the dependencies and builds Code Mode when its `dist` is missing or stale. It configures the `.utcp_config.json` manuals. It registers Code Mode in the seven project config files, two of them for Claude. Each write waits for its own approval. Add `--runtime <name>` to register one runtime only: `opencode`, `claude`, `codex`, `cursor`, `pi` or `devin`.\n105: \n106: To check Code Mode later, run `/doctor:mcp debug`. It reports PASS, WARN or FAIL per check, and `--fix` adds guided repair. Provider keys live in `.env`, which only Code Mode reads. Section 1 of [`.env.example`](.env.example) lists them. An agent cannot read that file: the [dotenv guard](.skilled/hooks/env-secret-guard/README.md) refuses it on every runtime and offers key names or a fingerprint instead. The [Code Mode install guide](.skilled/skills/mcp-code-mode/INSTALL-GUIDE.md) covers the manual steps.\n107: \n108: ### Git Hooks\n109: \n110: The first AI session installs the repository's git hooks. The root README's [Git Hooks](README.md#git-hooks) section says what each hook blocks. `/doctor:git setup` checks the hooks and the sk-git settings.\n111: \n112: Your checkout is ready once `/doctor:setup` passes. Run `/doctor:setup` again whenever something looks wrong. To use Skilled in a project of your own, pick a way in below.\n113: \n114: ---\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 115]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/INSTALL-SKILLED.md\",\"offset\":45,\"limit\":70}"},"time":{"start":1794539492105,"end":1794539492631}}}}
